---
import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';

const toc = [
  { id: 'current-status', label: 'Current status' },
  { id: 'observation', label: 'What we observed' },
  { id: 'upstream-change', label: 'What changed upstream' },
  { id: 'verification', label: 'How we checked it' },
  { id: 'scope', label: 'Scope' },
  { id: 'verification-relation', label: 'Relation to verification' },
  { id: 'public-evidence', label: 'Public evidence' },
];
---

<PublicationLayout
  category="Engineering"
  type="Upstream report"
  title="A Julia signed-rank p-value above 1, now fixed in a release"
  description="HypothesisTests.jl returned 1.25 for an exact two-sided signed-rank p-value. The matching correction shipped in v0.12.0 and remains in v0.12.1."
  published="September 3, 2026"
  updated="September 9, 2026"
  status="Matching fix released in v0.12.0; present in v0.12.1; issue open"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="current-status">
    <h2>Current status</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        We reported HypothesisTests.jl issue #373 on August 25, 2026 after reproducing
        an exact two-sided signed-rank p-value above 1. Pull request #377 was opened by
        <code>yoninazarathy</code> on September 2 and merged into <code>master</code> by
        <code>andreasnoack</code> later that day.
      </p>
      <p>
        As of September 9, 2026, the matching correction has shipped in
        <code>v0.12.0</code> (September 3) and remains in the latest registered version,
        <code>v0.12.1</code> (September 4). Issue #373 remains open. The public tracker
        does not state that #373 caused #377, so this page does not make that attribution.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="observation">
    <h2>What we observed</h2>
    <p>
      In HypothesisTests.jl <code>0.11.8</code> on Julia <code>1.12.7</code>, the
      untied exact path returned <code>1.25</code> for this call:
    </p>
    <pre><code>pvalue(ExactSignedRankTest([1.0, 2.0, -3.0]))</code></pre>
    <p>
      A p-value is a probability, so it must remain between 0 and 1. For this
      three-observation example, exhaustive enumeration of the eight possible sign
      assignments gives two one-sided probabilities of <code>5/8</code>. The standard
      doubled-tail value is therefore <code>min(1, 2 × 5/8) = 1</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="upstream-change">
    <h2>What changed upstream</h2>
    <p>
      Pull request #377 changed the no-ties exact two-sided branch so the selected
      tail is doubled and capped at <code>1.0</code>. The merged regression test pins
      the example above to exactly <code>1</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pull request also sweeps every untied sign pattern through sample size
      <code>n = 12</code> and asserts that no two-sided result exceeds 1. Its author
      reported that 220 of 8,190 swept patterns exceeded 1 before the patch.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="verification">
    <h2>How we checked it</h2>
    <p>
      We ran the same fixture in clean Julia <code>1.12.7</code> environments against
      two pinned package states. Registered HypothesisTests.jl <code>0.11.8</code>
      returned <code>1.25</code>; merge commit <code>f758eea</code> returned
      <code>1.0</code>. Both checks used assertions on the displayed result rather than
      relying only on source inspection.
    </p>
    <p>
      On September 9, we checked the release records, General registry, and v0.12.1
      source, which retains <code>min(2 * p, 1.0)</code>. This confirms release inclusion
      by source inspection; the execution results below remain the original pinned runs.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Package state</th>
            <th>Observed p-value</th>
            <th>Status</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Registered version <code>0.11.8</code></td>
            <td><code>1.25</code></td>
            <td>Released behavior reproduced</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Merge commit <code>f758eea</code></td>
            <td><code>1.0</code></td>
            <td>Matching fix reproduced</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scope">
    <h2>Scope</h2>
    <p>
      This report concerns the untied, no-zero, exact two-sided signed-rank path in the
      tested HypothesisTests.jl versions. It does not show that every signed-rank call
      is affected, and it does not estimate how often ordinary research workflows
      reach a statistic at the null-distribution center.
    </p>
    <p>
      Version <code>0.11.8</code> predates the fix; v0.12.0 and v0.12.1 contain it.
      Release inclusion does not establish correctness for every rank-test path.
      Our report and the separately authored patch remain distinct because the
      upstream record does not establish a causal link.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="verification-relation">
    <h2>Relation to verification</h2>
    <p>
      The case shows the value of checking simple mathematical invariants alongside
      high-precision reference values. A range assertion identified an invalid result;
      exhaustive enumeration explained the expected answer; and a pinned cross-version
      run separated released behavior from the current upstream fix.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is evidence of Licklider's verification engineering method. It does not mean
      that nomue Protocol currently supports Julia or signed-rank verification.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="public-evidence">
    <h2>Public evidence</h2>
    <ul>
